### ✅ **C Solution**

```c
/**
 * Definition for a binary tree node.
 * struct TreeNode {
 *     int val;
 *     struct TreeNode *left;
 *     struct TreeNode *right;
 * };
 */

bool isSameTree(struct TreeNode* p, struct TreeNode* q) {
    // If both are NULL, they’re identical so far
    if (p == NULL && q == NULL)
        return true;

    // If one is NULL and the other isn’t, structure differs
    if (p == NULL || q == NULL)
        return false;

    // If values differ, not the same tree
    if (p->val != q->val)
        return false;

    // Recursively check left and right subtrees
    return isSameTree(p->left, q->left) && isSameTree(p->right, q->right);
}
```

---

### 💬 Logic Breakdown
1. If both trees are empty → ✅ same.  
2. If one is empty → ❌ not same.  
3. If current node values differ → ❌ not same.  
4. Otherwise → recursively compare their left and right children.  

